[{:db/id 17592186124414 :question/problem "Consider the matrix ![](images/4e/4e737b78109f94302bdc256b4a8bb9585fdb214ccc89bd9439b52fa4973f59ba.svg) You're given that $\\lambda_1=4$ and $\\lambda_2=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A,$ and\n![](images/6b/6b4045bf6fc71339953a55a1e3660384a569861537ee37427be110aa3442a872.svg) ![](images/34/34760505c150775e9b579151b333cf3baa044102e0197b4e9aae3444c0581cd0.svg) are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4}$ and $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3},$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n![](images/42/427a788b1c9f78e8ce25f06b54cef549d7be04757f6275cc66f12bd48652e40b.svg)\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4},$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3}.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n![](images/bd/bdf233e22e7972a4eb912463bc81727d7e22b160ff823ec51d0be494d24d493c.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124411 :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n![](images/af/af05cedf7b2015f4f61df71e833390aa845d50126c3203369320ac6feb8b2cc3.svg)\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124412 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n![](images/6e/6e51c22c33c32d52c51657552a3c174d706a316ec10c78a4a953d2fcebc304ff.svg)\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix ![](images/0e/0e25fe1b46b828e6fac168d1755af7aed9a7c3f657d1a6d6677fd71746b7ce31.svg) we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1= -{2}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/54/54dad1c9d747fead6f27dd0e9af7cca434c40a83d56c02c854bb610a1cdc6087.svg)\n\nBy setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/d2/d28cf572126716928bc9ded7c2646871540e20b1d93d1efc929599b4c379d38b.svg)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-2}{1} = -2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124413 :question/problem "Given the matrix \n![](images/3d/3da867d461c2b42c21badd67ce59c9ec5f6448597b4d2dcbb7e8b28ffe397768.svg) \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm. \n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\quad\\quad\\lambda_2=-2.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{8}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/04/04f6efaa2bb141940040a04905e52aacd7aa739e1bd58dd71666a10543ff0f74.svg)\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector ![](images/dd/dd78cbf2025fdf012dc27429596c84be09825b436c32a5410f50f78cd4943a66.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix corresponds to a homogeneous system of linear equations. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{8}{5}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/5b/5b5b06d4cd243ca0370807b58bc9d0da68f34aa008956016bab3da29377df219.svg)\nSetting  $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector  ![](images/3e/3ea698aa474cb6d28b3fb511215fbe5da7d01b6653e884719d99483eac5635a0.svg)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n![](images/b7/b736228d68ad58b430f707236014be76e0354bf42ff9fcd73c495a077835bad7.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125839 :tutorial/content "A square matrix $A$ is called **diagonalizable** if there exists a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA = PDP^{-1}.\n$$\n\nSo, to **diagonalize** a matrix $A,$ we need to find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ that satisfy the equation above.\n\nFor example, let's diagonalize the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/9d/9dee6d9184932a79f36305b9fca2bbb5a2a18bc79b49ad97038f780a2f125e49.svg)\n\nTo diagonalize a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we follow a three-step **diagonalization algorithm.**\n\n**Step 1:** Find the eigenvalues of the matrix.\n\nWe solve the characteristic equation $\\vert A - \\lambda I \\vert = 0.$ For the given matrix, the eigenvalues are $\\color{red}\\lambda_1 =2$ and $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2 =3.$\n\n**Step 2:** Find a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of eigenvectors of the matrix.\n\nFollowing the usual method for computing eigenvectors, we get \n\n![](images/ca/caa7716ce45eef38d92092d27827d30fd539fca27067c1e12f485a719b4f37ae.svg)\n\ncorresponding to the eigenvalues $\\color{red}\\lambda_1 =2$ and $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2 =3,$ respectively.\n\nNote that $\\left\\{ {\\color{red}\\mathbf{v_1}}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_2}}\\right\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since eigenvectors corresponding to distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent. This becomes important later.\n\n**Step 3: ** Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nNow that we have our eigenvalues and corresponding eigenvectors, we can construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and invertible matrix $P.$\n\nThe matrix $D$ has the eigenvalues of the matrix as its entries on the diagonal. For now, we can write them in any order. So, we let\n\n![](images/3e/3e0df792d822773528cb07cceb39fcb98b7397ae70ab4f114e1450f1d91256d1.svg)\n\nThen, the columns of $P$ are the corresponding eigenvectors. We need to write each eigenvector in the *same* column as its respective eigenvalue in the matrix $D.$ So, we get\n\n![](images/86/869a05b6273dbbb1b53d9f842d8b2c612017517a0b007c1c5f494f55439a11b4.svg)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=PD{P}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check our solution by computing the product on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125840 :tutorial/content "There is a theorem that states the following:\n\n*If $A$ is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, then it is diagonalizable if and only if there exists a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ that consists of eigenvectors of $A.$*\n\nWe can break this down into two possible cases:\n\n- If $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues, then it is indeed diagonalizable. This is because two distinct eigenvalues will give rise to two linearly independent eigenvectors (one for each eigenvalue). \n\nSo, for a matrix $A$ with eigenvalues $\\color{red}\\lambda_1$ and $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2$ and corresponding eigenvectors $\\color{red}\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_2},$ we have\n![](images/e1/e1468bc5ccdff43e33587c7b4b500bc527ab0ac7e74410ea53df1f803a19b520.svg)\n\n- If we get a double root $\\lambda$ of the characteristic equation, the matrix will be diagonalizable *only if* we can find exactly two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda,$ which may or may not be possible. Another way of saying this is that the corresponding eigenspace $V_\\lambda$ must be of dimension $2.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125842 :tutorial/content "Given a matrix $A,$ the diagonalization \n\n![](images/76/76843143729d2bc62fc74dc6f03c24eb6a12dfc16a2609a8f2b36499cf39ce90.svg)\n\nis almost unique, but not quite.\n\nWhen we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ we can put the eigenvalues in any order. So this matrix is unique up to permutations of the order of the eigenvalues. Note that by swapping the eigenvalues in $D,$ we then have to swap the corresponding eigenvectors (columns) in $P{:}$\n\n![](images/de/dec5fece3d89eaa022b4e951e63259b0f87213997397b1f8a91fb8fdabed0f74.svg)\n\nAlso, since any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is an eigenvector as well, the eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ can consist of these multiples instead. So the invertible matrix $P$ is unique up to multiples of its columns." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124753 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
